tests:
  unit: { count: 70, covers: "The address refusal and the interface filter (loopback, link-local and the unspecified address are never offered, private and global addresses are); the certificate URL built from the bound API socket, brackets kept for IPv6; Reach defaults to Local and an armed config does not move the local bind; the three /reach handlers' shapes - null addresses when unarmed, a non-IP address refused before any bind is attempted, an idempotent disarm; /reach present in the router's route-coverage list; the typed client's three calls, the body the arm sends and the server sentence a refusal throws; the shell's confirm message, which must name the address, say the captured flows become readable and not merely the proxy usable, and say the exposure ends with the session." }
  integration: { count: 9, covers: "The WU contract end to end over ephemeral ports: a default core binds loopback only and promises no address; arming binds a second proxy and control API at the reported address, serves /ca.crt there and lands that proxy's traffic in the same capture; the unspecified address is refused and leaves nothing armed; an address the host does not hold is refused by the bind; disarm removes the pair while loopback keeps serving; a second arm replaces rather than accumulates; the four CORS grant headers are identical over both addresses; a configured reach boots armed and shutdown takes its listeners with it. The second listener is bound on loopback - no test touches the public network." }
  e2e: { waived: "The epic plan puts the e2e at W4, where it drives the screen W3 rebuilds. This WU renders nothing, so an e2e here would have no surface to drive; its core seam is covered at integration level instead, which is what the epic plan's no-waiver clause asks of this WU." }
